# Running one

The Deno reference relay is a single file you point at a port:

```sh
deno run --allow-net --allow-ffi --allow-env --allow-read \
    bindings/ts/relay/serve.ts 9000
# DENO-RELAY-READY 9000
```

It relays wherever a WebSocket points it, named by `?to=` or `?locator=`, and
reclaims a silent session once its idle timeout passes (`PANCAKE_RELAY_IDLE_MS`,
one minute by default). Target allowlists and admission caps are left to the
embedding app. The rooms relay
([`relay.ts`](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/apps/rooms/web/relay.ts))
shows how an embedding adds target policy and admission limits. The
[relay README](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/bindings/ts/relay/README.md)
covers the package itself, and the Python, Rust, and Racket relays take the
same relay contract.

The [rooms]($link.page('examples')) chat and its browser direct messages run
over a real relay, and [the TypeScript binding]($link.page('bindings/ts')) is
where the browser peer lives.
